---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import sins from '../../data/sins.json';
import { SITE, ARTICLE_AUTHOR } from '../../data/site';

const title = 'Seven Deadly Sins and Virtues: The Seven Contrary Virtues Explained';
const description =
  'Which virtue opposes each deadly sin? A complete seven-by-seven table pairing pride with humility, envy with kindness, wrath with patience, and the rest, with explanations.';

const pairNotes: Record<string, string> = {
  pride:
    'Humility is an honest account of your own limits and debts, so wins can be shared and "I was wrong" said without feeling diminished.',
  envy:
    'Kindness actively wills good for the person whose advantage stings, so admiration replaces the resentment before it sets.',
  wrath:
    'Patience holds the gap between a trigger and a response, keeping anger a tool you use rather than a master that uses you.',
  sloth:
    'Diligence applies small, steady effort before motivation arrives, refusing avoidance without refusing scheduled rest.',
  greed:
    'Charity releases money, time, credit, and information on purpose, before you feel you can spare them.',
  gluttony:
    'Temperance decides how much is enough in advance and stops there, applied to food, drink, screens, and the phone.',
  lust:
    'Chastity integrates desire with your commitments and respect for others, treating attraction as information rather than a command.',
};

const articleLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  author: ARTICLE_AUTHOR,
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url + '/' },
  datePublished: '2026-11-19',
  dateModified: '2026-11-19',
  mainEntityOfPage: new URL('/seven-deadly-sins-and-virtues/', SITE.url).href,
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/seven-deadly-sins-and-virtues/"
  type="article"
  publishedTime="2026-11-19"
  jsonLd={articleLd}
>
  <Breadcrumbs
    items={[
      { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
      { name: 'Sins & Virtues', path: '/seven-deadly-sins-and-virtues/' },
    ]}
  />

  <article class="prose-article mt-4">
    <h1>Seven Deadly Sins and Virtues: Which Virtue Opposes Which Sin?</h1>
    <p>
      The medieval moral tradition did not leave the seven deadly sins without remedies. Each sin
      is matched with a <strong>contrary virtue</strong> &mdash; a trained habit that pulls
      character in the opposite direction. The pairing is practical rather than poetic: if a sin is
      a groove worn into behavior by repetition, the contrary virtue is a new groove cut beside it.
      This page gives you the complete seven-by-seven table in one glance &mdash; each sin linked
      to its full guide, each virtue linked to the practice that countertrains it. For the full
      definition and history of each sin, start with the
      <a href="/seven-deadly-sins/"> seven deadly sins guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The Seven Sins and Their Contrary Virtues: Table</h2>
    <table>
      <caption class="text-left mb-2 text-[var(--color-muted)]">
        Table 1: each deadly sin paired with its contrary heavenly virtue.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Deadly sin</th>
          <th scope="col">Latin name</th>
          <th scope="col">Contrary virtue</th>
          <th scope="col">What the virtue looks like in practice</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          sins.map((sin) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                <a href={`/seven-deadly-sins/${sin.slug}/`}>{sin.name}</a>
              </th>
              <td>{sin.latin}</td>
              <td>
                <strong>
                  <a href={`/seven-heavenly-virtues/#${sin.virtue.toLowerCase()}`}>{sin.virtue}</a>
                </strong>
              </td>
              <td>{pairNotes[sin.slug]}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Where the Contrary Virtues Come From</h2>
    <p>
      Pairing every vice with a remedial virtue is older than the seven-item list it now serves.
      The instinct runs back to Greek ethics, where Aristotle described virtue as a trained habit
      lying between two extremes, and it entered Christian moral teaching through the desert monks
      who treated each recurring thought as a condition to be countered rather than merely
      forbidden. By the time Pope Gregory I fixed the seven deadly sins around 590 CE, the matching
      remedies were already circulating as a teaching device: a vice named precisely enough to be
      recognized could also be answered by a named practice.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two features of the pairing are easy to miss. First, it is remedial rather than cosmetic
      &mdash; each contrary virtue is chosen because it targets the mechanism of its sin, not
      because the word sounds opposite. Pride protects a ranking, so humility restores accurate
      self-knowledge; greed fears insufficiency, so charity supplies the counter-evidence that
      enough already exists. Second, the seven contrary virtues are not the same set as the four
      cardinal virtues joined to the three theological ones. This page covers the contrary-virtues
      set specifically, because it is the one that maps one-to-one onto the deadly sins and can be
      read straight across the table above.
    </p>

    <h2>How to Use the Pairings</h2>
    <p>
      The useful way to read this table is not as a pass-fail judgment but as a training menu.
      Take the result from the <a href="/">homepage seven deadly sins quiz</a>, identify your
      dominant dimension, and practice its contrary virtue deliberately for a few weeks &mdash;
      one specific, repeatable action rather than a vague intention. Because virtues are skills,
      measurable change usually shows up before it feels real. When you retake the quiz afterward,
      the percentage profile shifts; that shift is the tradition&rsquo;s own feedback loop,
      modernized.
    </p>
    <p>
      Return to the <a href="/seven-deadly-sins/">seven deadly sins overview</a> for origins,
      definitions, modern examples, and misconceptions, or browse the remedy side directly in the
      guide to the <a href="/seven-heavenly-virtues/">seven heavenly virtues</a> and the{' '}
      <a href="/seven-deadly-sins/list/">seven deadly sins list</a> in traditional order. To put
      the framework to work: take the fifteen-question <a href="/">seven deadly sins test</a> to
      find your dominant sin, then take the matching{' '}
      <a href="/seven-virtues-quiz/">seven heavenly virtues quiz</a> to see which corrective virtue
      is already your strongest.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
